{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-backend","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What class must a custom Django template backend inherit from, and which method(s) must it implement?","answer":"It must inherit django.template.backends.base.BaseEngine and implement get_template(); implementing from_string() is optional."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-backend","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What value does the example FooBar template backend use for app_dirname?","answer":"\"foobar\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-backend","fact_refs":[3],"question":"When does the Django template postmortem debug page appear?","answer":"It appears when django.template.TemplateDoesNotExist is raised."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-backend","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What two arguments should a custom template engine pass when raising TemplateDoesNotExist so it populates the postmortem page?","answer":"The backend and tried arguments."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-backend","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How can a custom template engine make Django show the exact line where a template error occurred?","answer":"By setting a template_debug attribute (a dict) on exceptions raised during template parsing or rendering."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-backend","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Roughly how many lines should the 'source_lines' entry in template_debug contain?","answer":"No more than about 20 lines, since it's only meant to provide context."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-backend","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What three attributes must an object provide to supply custom template.origin information for a Django template?","answer":"name (full path to the template), template_name (relative path as passed to the loader), and loader_name (identifying the loader used)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"Custom template tags/filters should live in a templatetags directory placed at the same level as models.py and views.py inside a Django app.","source_span":"The app should contain a ``templatetags`` directory, at the same level as\n``models.py``, ``views.py``, etc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"The templatetags directory needs an __init__.py file so it is treated as a Python package.","source_span":"don't forget the ``__init__.py`` file to ensure the directory is treated as a\nPython package."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"After adding the templatetags module, the development server must be restarted before the tags or filters can be used in templates.","source_span":"After adding the ``templatetags``  module, you will need to restart your\nserver before you can use the tags or filters in templates."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"The app containing custom tags must be listed in INSTALLED_APPS for the {% load %} tag to work.","source_span":"The app that contains the custom tags must be in :setting:`INSTALLED_APPS` in\norder for the :ttag:`{% load %}<load>` tag to work."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"To be a valid tag library, a module must contain a module-level variable named register that is a template.Library instance.","source_span":"To be a valid tag library, the module must contain a module-level variable\nnamed ``register`` that is a ``template.Library`` instance"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"Custom filters are Python functions that take one or two arguments: the variable's value and an optional argument value.","source_span":"Custom filters are Python functions that take one or two arguments:\n\n* The value of the variable (input) -- not necessarily a string.\n* The value of the argument"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"The Library.filter() method takes two arguments: the filter's name (a string) and the compilation function.","source_span":"The ``Library.filter()`` method takes two arguments:\n\n1. The name of the filter -- a string.\n2. The compilation function"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"register.filter() also accepts three keyword arguments: is_safe, needs_autoescape, and expects_localtime.","source_span":"``register.filter()`` also accepts three keyword arguments,\n``is_safe``, ``needs_autoescape``, and ``expects_localtime``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"If the name argument is left off when registering a filter, Django uses the function's own name as the filter name.","source_span":"If you leave off the ``name`` argument, as in the second example above, Django\nwill use the function's name as the filter name."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"The stringfilter decorator converts an object to its string value before it is passed to the filter function.","source_span":"This will\nconvert an object to its string value before being passed to your function"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","statement":"Template tag modules can alternatively be registered through the 'libraries' argument to django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates, which allows registering tags without installing an app.","source_span":"template tag modules can be registered through the\n``'libraries'`` argument to\n:class:`~django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates`... It also enables you to register tags without installing\nan application."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"Where inside a Django app should custom template tags and filters be placed, and what extra file does that location need?","answer":"In a templatetags directory at the same level as models.py and views.py; it also needs an __init__.py file so it's treated as a Python package."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","fact_refs":[2],"question":"After adding a new templatetags module to a Django app, what must you do before you can use the tags or filters in templates?","answer":"Restart the development server."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What condition must be satisfied for the {% load %} tag to be able to load an app's custom template tags?","answer":"The app that contains the custom tags must be listed in INSTALLED_APPS."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What must a Python module contain to be considered a valid Django template tag library?","answer":"A module-level variable named register that is an instance of template.Library."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What two arguments does Django's Library.filter() method take?","answer":"The filter's name (a string) and the compilation function (the actual Python function, not its name as a string)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What three optional keyword arguments does register.filter() accept?","answer":"is_safe, needs_autoescape, and expects_localtime."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_custom-template-tags","fact_refs":[10],"question":"Besides placing tags in an installed app's templatetags directory, how else can template tag modules be registered with Django?","answer":"Through the 'libraries' argument to django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates, which also lets you register tags without installing an app."}
